# TinyACE v3 rules

This repo produces numbers for a paper. A bug that changes a number is worse than a crash.
Follow these rules on every task. If a task asks you to break one, stop and say so.

## Stop conditions
- If the code does not match what the prompt describes (file moved, function renamed, issue
  already fixed), STOP and report what you found. Do not guess, and do not fix something else.
- If a fix needs a decision the prompt does not make (a threshold, a default, prompt wording
  shown to a model, a file format), STOP and ask. Never invent research parameters.
- Never edit `configs/hypotheses.yaml`, `configs/teacher_qualification.yaml`, screening
  constants in `utils/config.py`, or any pre-registered threshold unless the prompt says so.

## Measurement integrity
- lm-evaluation-harness produces every reported accuracy (`run_frozen_eval`). Project code
  never computes a reported accuracy itself.
- Item ids come ONLY from the frozen manifests in `data/splits/` (via `frozen_split()` /
  `manifest_ids()` once they exist). `--seed` controls randomness, never which items are used.
- Never regenerate, re-sample or hand-edit `data/splits/*.json`. Never run
  `make splits-REGENERATE` or `freeze_splits --force`. A NEW manifest only when a prompt says so.
- The adaptation loop must never see an evaluation item: every production call to
  `adapt_playbook` passes the real evaluation manifest.
- Adaptation and evaluation render prompts identically. Any prompt-rendering change keeps
  `assert_matches_harness()` and the byte-identity tests passing.
- Prompt truncation invalidates a run; never truncate or drop lessons silently.
- Token counts use the study model's own tokenizer with `add_special_tokens=False`, never
  `words * 1.3`.
- A run with `--limit` is a debug run and must never be aggregated.

## Repo conventions
- Results paths go through `reporting/layout.py` only. Arms are registered in
  `reporting/schema.py` before any runner uses them. Health rules live in `reporting/health.py`.
- Text shown to a model (scaffolds, reflector/curator/guide prompts) lives in files under
  `prompts/` so the paper can quote it exactly.
- Keep the existing docstring style: explain WHY a line exists, especially when it guards a
  past bug. Don't delete those explanations; update them.
- Keep the CI smoke tests in `.github/workflows/ci.yaml` working (`run_arm` with `tiny-gpt2`).

## Teacher model
- All teacher calls go through the teacher client and its on-disk cache; everything is
  archived under `artifacts/teacher/`. Evaluation runs in replay mode (no network).
- API keys come from environment variables. Never write a key, token or `.env` file into the repo.

## Engineering
- `pyproject.toml` is the only packaging source. Use the project `.venv`; never the system or
  Homebrew Python.
- Write the failing test first, then the fix. Unit tests never touch the network or HF cache;
  tests that do are marked `@pytest.mark.network`.
- Keep `flake8`, `black --check --target-version py311 src/ scripts/ tests/`,
  `python -m scripts.freeze_splits --check` and `pytest -m "not network"` passing.
- Small diffs. No unrelated refactors, renames or formatting sweeps.
- When done, print: files changed, tests added, the commands you ran and their results, and a
  suggested commit message. The human commits after every prompt.
